Below is a comprehensive guide on building a shopping cart component using React with state management handled by Redux Toolkit. This will include a simple list of items (think items you could add to a cart), actions to add/remove these items, and a display of the current count of items in the cart. We want our shopping cart to be able to store its state in memory so we can persist the state across different routes. So we setup Redux and Redux Toolkit in our React app.

### Prerequisites

Make sure you have set up a basic React project with either `create-react-app` or started one manually. I recommend `create-react-app` for ease.

Then, you need Redux Toolkit which can easily get installed thanks to the `@reduxjs/toolkit` package. Install Redux and Redux Toolkit using npm or yarn:

```bash
npm install react-redux @reduxjs/toolkit
```

### Step 1: Enhance the Redux Store with Redux Toolkit

**src/reducers/cartSlice.js**
```javascript
import { createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';

const initialState = {
  items: [],
  itemCount: 0,
};

const cartSlice = createSlice({
  name: 'cart',
  initialState,
  reducers: {
    addItem(state, action) {
      const existingItem = state.items.find(
        (item) => item.id === action.payload.id
      );
      if (existingItem) {
        existingItem.quantity++;
      } else {
        state.items.push({ id: action.payload.id, quantity: 1 });
        state.itemCount++;
      }
    },
    removeItem(state, action) {
      state.items = state.items.filter((item) => item.id!== action.payload.id);
      state.itemCount--;
    },
  },
});

export const { addItem, removeItem } = cartSlice.actions;
export default cartSlice.reducer;
```

